---
// Ordinal's story, from Francisco's migration notes (2026-10-07); the
// migration pull request's numbers are checked against GitHub.
import { NEW_TAB_ATTRS } from "blume/core/new-tab.ts";
import Quote from "../_customers/Quote.astro";
import StoryPage from "../_customers/StoryPage.astro";
import { storyById } from "../_customers/customers.ts";
---

<StoryPage story={storyById("ordinal")}>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.tryordinal.com" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>Ordinal</a> is a
    social media management platform for B2B teams. It's built around
    LinkedIn: teams draft and schedule posts for executives, company pages, and
    employees, route them through approvals, coordinate likes, comments, and
    reposts with{" "}
    <a
      href="https://docs.tryordinal.com/posts/team-engagements"
      {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}>Team Engagements</a
    >, and track performance in one analytics view. Ordinal also publishes to
    X, Instagram, Threads, Facebook, TikTok, YouTube Shorts, Webflow, Slack,
    and Discord. For developers and AI assistants, it offers a REST API, an{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.tryordinal.com/mcp/introduction" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}
      >MCP server</a
    >, and webhooks.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its docs cover all of it: a guide for every feature, references for two
    APIs, and a page for every webhook event.
  </p>

  <h2>How the docs started</h2>
  <p>
    Ordinal's docs started on Mintlify, and Mintlify got them off the ground
    fast. Francisco's co-founder, Jeffrey Zhao, signed the team up, and though
    Jeffrey isn't an engineer, within a few days and with a $200 Cursor
    subscription he had fed Mintlify all of Ordinal's product context and stood
    up solid docs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mintlify's agent became part of how the team worked. It opened pull
    requests to update the docs as the product changed, and wrote 46 of the
    last 60 docs changes Ordinal merged before the move.
  </p>
  <p>
    Day to day, the team worked in Git. The docs were MDX in their own
    repository from the start, and changes went through GitHub pull requests,
    so a hosted editor wasn't something Ordinal needed. With the yearly renewal
    coming up, Francisco looked at moving the docs to a framework the team could
    run itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The move</h2>
  <p>
    He'd had his eye on Blume for a few months. One evening in
    September 2026, he ran <code>npx blume migrate mintlify</code>, which found
    Ordinal's <code>docs.json</code> and handed the job to Claude Code with
    Blume's migration playbook. Its bundled codemod rewrote the frontmatter and
    icons on 68 pages in one pass, and Claude worked through the rest unattended
    while Francisco walked the dog.
  </p>
  <p>
    The content moved almost one to one. Cards (about 250 of them), Steps, Tabs,
    Accordions, Expandables, CodeGroups, and Frames all have Blume equivalents
    with nothing to import, and callouts became <code>:::</code> directives.
    The 273 response fields on the 22 webhook event pages became type tables.
  </p>
  <p>
    The real work was keeping URLs. Mintlify's sidebar groups lived only in{" "}
    <code>docs.json</code>, so Claude rebuilt them as{" "}
    <a href="/docs/content/navigation#folder-groups">group folders</a> like{" "}
    <code>(calendar-and-posts)/</code>, which add no segment to the URL, and
    gave pages that changed folders a <code>slug</code> that kept their old
    address. The Guides tab sits at the site root, so nothing had to move under
    a new prefix. Not one guide URL changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    The API reference came for free: pointing{" "}
    <a href="/docs/references/openapi"><code>openapi()</code></a> at Ordinal's
    two existing specs built 72 endpoint pages with schemas, code samples, and
    a Try it panel. Blume names endpoint pages by their operation ID, where
    Mintlify used each one's summary, so 72 of the site's 79{" "}
    <a href="/docs/deployment#redirects">redirects</a> send old endpoint URLs
    to their new pages. On Vercel, they're real 301s.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before cutover,{" "}
    <a href="/docs/cli/validate"><code>blume validate</code></a> checked every
    link and redirect. Its first run flagged 52 broken links, which exposed 19
    pages a content glob had silently left out of the build. With those back,
    the site went live on Vercel the next day.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything else was a line or two in <code>blume.config.ts</code>:{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/llms-txt">llms.txt</a>, a{" "}
    <a href="/docs/discoverability/markdown">Markdown version</a> of every page,
    a <a href="/docs/discoverability/mcp">docs MCP server</a> beside Ordinal's
    own product MCP server, the{" "}
    <a href="/docs/configuration/assistant">assistant</a>, PostHog analytics,
    and Edit on GitHub links.
  </p>

  <Quote
    avatar="/customers/francisco.webp"
    name="Francisco"
    role="Co-founder, Ordinal"
  >
    I started the migration and went to walk the dog. About 20 minutes of my
    time, and every URL still worked.
  </Quote>

  <h2>Docs that keep themselves current</h2>
  <p>
    Mintlify's agent had been writing most of the docs changes, so Ordinal
    rebuilt that job as a scheduled Claude Code routine. Every weekday at 5pm
    Chicago time, a cloud
    Claude Code session checks out both the product and the docs repositories,
    then:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Lists the product pull requests merged in the last seven days, skipping
      any that a docs pull request already references, so it never repeats
      work that's already documented.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reads each one's description and diff and decides whether customers would
      notice. Bug fixes, refactors, backfills, internal automations, and work
      behind feature flags are skipped.
    </li>
    <li>
      Writes the docs change following the repository's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>,
      which spells out Blume's syntax and conventions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Runs <code>npm run build</code> and <code>npm run validate</code>, and
      goes on only if both pass with no errors.
    </li>
    <li>Opens one pull request per change for a person to review and merge.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The routine needs only git, the GitHub CLI, and npm, so it runs with no
    connectors attached. It reads pull request text that other people wrote,
    and keeping email, Slack, and payments out of its reach limits what an
    injected instruction could do.
  </p>

  <h2>The results</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide URL survived the move, the API references build from
    Ordinal's own specs, and the docs keep themselves current through pull
    requests the team reviews.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">What</th>
        <th scope="col">Number</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Hand-written pages</th><td>80, across 5 header tabs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">API reference pages</th><td>72, from 2 OpenAPI specs</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Redirects</th><td>79, 72 of them for endpoints</td>
      </tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Guide URLs changed</th><td>0</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Webhook fields converted</th><td>273, on 22 event pages</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">Francisco's hands-on time</th><td>About 20 minutes</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The whole move is one{" "}
    <a href="https://github.com/ordinalhq/docs/pull/145" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}
      >public pull request</a
    >, and the new docs are live at{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.tryordinal.com" {...NEW_TAB_ATTRS}
      >docs.tryordinal.com</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    To make the same move, follow the{" "}
    <a href="/guides/migrate-from-mintlify">Mintlify migration guide</a>.
  </p>
</StoryPage>
